---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Perception Lab | tcplot: Data Visualization in Minneapolis and St. Paul"
    description="Replicate classic visual-perception experiments, run them on the room, and chart our own error rates — the morning the attendees are the dataset."
>
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8">
        <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mt-8 mb-12">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">
                Upcoming: Perception Lab
            </h2>

            <div class="mb-6 text-gray-700">
                <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">
                    Saturday, Nov 21 · 10:00 AM to 12:00 PM CST
                </p>
                <p class="flex items-start gap-2 mt-1">
                    <svg
                        class="w-5 h-5 mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-gray-400"
                        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                        fill="currentColor"
                        aria-hidden="true"
                    >
                        <path
                            fill-rule="evenodd"
                            d="M12 2c-3.866 0-7 3.07-7 6.857 0 4.34 4.686 9.86 6.412 11.74a.8.8 0 0 0 1.176 0C14.314 18.717 19 13.197 19 8.857 19 5.07 15.866 2 12 2Zm0 9.5a2.5 2.5 0 1 1 0-5 2.5 2.5 0 0 1 0 5Z"
                            clip-rule="evenodd"></path>
                    </svg>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >The Coffee Shop NE</span
                        ><br />2852 Johnson St NE · Minneapolis, MN</span
                    >
                </p>
            </div>

            <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                This time, the attendees are the dataset.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Join <span class="font-silkscreen">tcplot</span> to replicate the
                classic experiments of visual perception. Pick one from the menu,
                build it as a small web app, then everyone rotates through everyone
                else's station as a subject. We close by charting the room's own error
                rates against the published results — part coding workshop, part psychology
                experiment, part reveal party.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                The vintage cabinet: print-and-post classics
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Free to print and post around the shop as micro-stations. Each
                was a measuring instrument before it was a poster.
            </p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-8">
                <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-4">
                    <div class="flex justify-center items-center h-48 mb-3">
                        <img
                            src="/images/duck-rabbit-1892.png"
                            alt="The 1892 duck–rabbit drawing: an ambiguous animal head readable as either a duck facing left or a rabbit facing right"
                            class="max-h-full max-w-full object-contain"
                        />
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
                        <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >The Duck–Rabbit (1892).</span
                        > From <em>Fliegende Blätter</em>; Jastrow's and
                        Wittgenstein's favorite ambiguous figure. Measure: what
                        people see first, and seconds to the first flip.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-4">
                    <div class="flex justify-center items-center h-48 mb-3">
                        <img
                            src="/images/wife-and-mother-in-law-1915.jpg"
                            alt="W. E. Hill's 1915 cartoon readable as either a young woman looking away or an old woman in profile"
                            class="max-h-full max-w-full object-contain"
                        />
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
                        <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >My Wife and My Mother-in-Law (1915).</span
                        > W. E. Hill, for <em>Puck</em>. Measure: which face
                        appears first, against viewer age — younger observers
                        tend to meet the young woman first.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-4">
                    <div class="flex justify-center items-center h-48 mb-3">
                        <img
                            src="/images/necker-cube-1832.svg"
                            alt="A Necker cube: a wireframe cube drawing whose front and back faces spontaneously swap"
                            class="max-h-full max-w-full object-contain"
                        />
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
                        <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >The Necker cube (1832).</span
                        > Necker noticed his crystal drawings flipping. Measure: flips
                        per minute in a sixty-second stare.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-4">
                    <div class="flex justify-center items-center h-48 mb-3">
                        <img
                            src="/images/zollner-illusion-1860.svg"
                            alt="The Zöllner illusion: long parallel lines crossed with short hatching that makes them appear to tilt"
                            class="max-h-full max-w-full object-contain"
                        />
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
                        <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >The Zöllner illusion (1860).</span
                        > The long lines are parallel; the hatching insists otherwise.
                        Measure: rotate one line until it looks parallel — the leftover
                        angle is the illusion.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-4">
                    <div class="flex justify-center items-center h-48 mb-3">
                        <img
                            src="/images/hermann-grid-1870.svg"
                            alt="The Hermann grid: black squares in a grid with white streets where gray dots appear to flicker at the intersections"
                            class="max-h-full max-w-full object-contain"
                        />
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
                        <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >The Hermann grid (1870).</span
                        > Ghost dots at the intersections that vanish where you look
                        directly. Measure: how far from fixation they appear.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-4">
                    <div class="flex justify-center items-center h-48 mb-3">
                        <img
                            src="/images/muller-lyer-illusion-1889.svg"
                            alt="The Müller-Lyer illusion: two equal lines that look unequal because one has inward and one has outward arrowheads"
                            class="max-h-full max-w-full object-contain"
                        />
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
                        <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >The Müller-Lyer illusion (1889).</span
                        > The shafts are identical. Measure: how much longer the fins-out
                        shaft must be before the pair looks equal.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-4">
                    <div class="flex justify-center items-center h-48 mb-3">
                        <img
                            src="/images/ponzo-illusion-1911.svg"
                            alt="The Ponzo illusion: two equal horizontal bars laid over converging lines, the upper bar appearing longer"
                            class="max-h-full max-w-full object-contain"
                        />
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
                        <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >The Ponzo illusion (1911).</span
                        > Equal bars over converging rails; the "farther" one looks
                        longer. Measure: shrink the upper bar until equal — what you
                        removed is the effect size.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-4">
                    <div class="flex justify-center items-center h-48 mb-3">
                        <img
                            src="/images/siemens-star-128-spokes.svg"
                            alt="A Siemens star: 128 black spokes radiating from a center point, fusing into gray toward the middle"
                            class="max-h-full max-w-full object-contain"
                        />
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
                        <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >The Siemens star.</span
                        > A 128-spoke resolution target — the acuity walk's instrument.
                        Hans Strasburger, <a
                            href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            >CC BY-SA 4.0</a
                        >, via <a
                            href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siemens_star_(128_spokes)_%26_Matlab_code.svg"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            >Wikimedia Commons</a
                        >.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                The flagship: Cleveland &amp; McGill
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                In 1984, Bell Labs statisticians Cleveland and McGill measured
                how accurately people read numbers back from charts — two bars,
                two pie slices, two shaded squares: "what percent is the smaller
                of the larger?" The resulting accuracy ranking still underpins
                chart design:
            </p>
            <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-600 mb-4">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        >Position on a common scale (bars, dot plots) — most
                        accurate</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span>Position on nonaligned scales, then length</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span>Angle (pie charts), then area (bubble charts)</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span>Shading and color saturation — close to guessing</span
                    >
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Heer &amp; Bostock got the same ranking on Mechanical Turk in
                2010. Does it hold in a coffee shop full of people who make
                charts on purpose?
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                How the morning will flow
            </h3>
            <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-600 mb-8">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Pick an experiment</span
                        > — solo or in teams.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Write the protocol</span
                        > — what's shown, what's asked, what's randomized — before
                        you write code.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Build it</span
                        > — a small web app that draws the stimulus, asks the question,
                        and logs each answer. AI assistants encouraged.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Test each other</span
                        > — everyone rotates through every station (except their own
                        — you know where the trick is).</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Chart &amp; present</span
                        > — three minutes per team: the classic finding, our room's
                        version, and where they differ.</span
                    >
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                Technical notes
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                We follow the original's method: stimuli drawn from the paper's
                ratios, errors scored as log<sub>2</sub>(|judged − true| + ⅛) so
                our numbers compare directly to 1984 and 2010, trial order
                randomized per subject, and all answers logged anonymously.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                The experiment menu
            </h3>
            <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-600 mb-4">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Gestalt grouping duel.</span
                        > Scatterplots where proximity, color, and connecting lines
                        disagree about the groups. The room's answers rank the Gestalt
                        principles.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Pop-out vs. hunting.</span
                        > Finding a red dot among blue is instant at any crowd size;
                        finding a red circle among red squares and blue circles gets
                        slower with each distractor. Plot time against set size —
                        the case for one highlight color, in one chart.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Change blindness.</span
                        > Two dashboards alternate with a blank flicker; one number
                        differs. Time how long people take to find it — routinely
                        tens of seconds.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Just-noticeable difference.</span
                        > How much taller must a bar be before people reliably see
                        it? A staircase procedure finds each person's threshold.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Ensemble perception.</span
                        > Flash a scatterplot for one second: people fail the count
                        but nail the mean.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >The area-guessing booth.</span
                        > How many times bigger is one circle than another? Stevens'
                        power law predicts systematic underestimation — the room's
                        own curve settles the bubble-chart argument.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >The acuity walk.</span
                        > The 128-spoke Siemens star above fuses to gray at the center;
                        the gray disk shrinks as you approach. Log distance against
                        the radius where spokes resolve.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Illusion corner.</span
                        > Printed classics from the cabinet above, each with a dataviz
                        moral.</span
                    >
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                The reading list
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                Each paper is the spec for its station:
            </p>
            <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-600 mb-6">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        >Cleveland &amp; McGill (1984), <a
                            href="https://doi.org/10.1080/01621459.1984.10478080"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            >"Graphical Perception"</a
                        >, <em>JASA</em></span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        >Heer &amp; Bostock (2010), <a
                            href="https://doi.org/10.1145/1753326.1753357"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            >"Crowdsourcing Graphical Perception"</a
                        >, <em>CHI</em></span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        >Wagemans et al. (2012), <a
                            href="https://doi.org/10.1037/a0029333"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            >"A Century of Gestalt Psychology in Visual
                            Perception"</a
                        >, <em>Psychological Bulletin</em></span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        >Treisman &amp; Gelade (1980), <a
                            href="https://doi.org/10.1016/0010-0285(80)90005-5"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            >"A Feature-Integration Theory of Attention"</a
                        >, <em>Cognitive Psychology</em></span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        >Rensink, O'Regan &amp; Clark (1997), <a
                            href="https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9280.1997.tb00427.x"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            >"To See or Not to See"</a
                        >, <em>Psychological Science</em></span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        >Stevens (1957), <a
                            href="https://doi.org/10.1037/h0046162"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            >"On the Psychophysical Law"</a
                        >, <em>Psychological Review</em></span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        >Szafir et al. (2016), <a
                            href="https://doi.org/10.1167/16.5.11"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            >"Four Types of Ensemble Coding in Data
                            Visualizations"</a
                        >, <em>Journal of Vision</em></span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        >Christopher Healey's interactive <a
                            href="https://www.csc2.ncsu.edu/faculty/healey/PP/"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            >Perception in Visualization</a
                        > page</span
                    >
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                And the shelf:
            </p>
            <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-600 mb-8">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        >Lidwell, Holden &amp; Butler, <a
                            href="https://search.worldcat.org/search?q=Universal+Principles+of+Design+Lidwell"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            ><em>Universal Principles of Design</em></a
                        ></span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        >Colin Ware, <a
                            href="https://search.worldcat.org/search?q=Information+Visualization+Perception+for+Design+Ware"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            ><em
                                >Information Visualization: Perception for
                                Design</em
                            ></a
                        > and <a
                            href="https://search.worldcat.org/search?q=Visual+Thinking+for+Design+Ware"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            ><em>Visual Thinking for Design</em></a
                        ></span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        >Tamara Munzner, <a
                            href="https://www.cs.ubc.ca/~tmm/vadbook/"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            ><em>Visualization Analysis and Design</em></a
                        ></span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        >William Cleveland, <a
                            href="https://search.worldcat.org/search?q=The+Elements+of+Graphing+Data+Cleveland"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            ><em>The Elements of Graphing Data</em></a
                        ></span
                    >
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                What to bring
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                A phone and a laptop.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                Who this is for
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Anyone who has ever repeated "position beats angle beats area"
                and wondered where it came from. You'll leave having built, run,
                and charted a real experiment in one morning.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                Why this matters for your charts
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                Every guideline in a chart style guide is one of these
                experiments frozen into a rule. Perception is the physics your
                charts run on:
            </p>
            <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-600 mb-6">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Cleveland &amp; McGill</span
                        > is why precision belongs in position: bars and dot plots
                        when the values matter, pies and heat-shading only when a
                        rough impression is enough.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900">Gestalt</span
                        > is why a connecting line makes points read as one series,
                        and why whitespace groups a legend better than a box around
                        it.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900">Pop-out</span
                        > is why one highlight color against gray carries the headline
                        — and why a second highlight color kills the first.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Change blindness</span
                        > is why a dashboard must announce what changed; viewers will
                        not notice on their own, however sure they are that they would.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Just-noticeable difference</span
                        > is why a difference the eye can't detect shouldn't be drawn
                        as a difference — annotate the number instead.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Ensemble perception</span
                        > is why showing the raw points often beats a summary chart:
                        the eye computes the mean for free.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Stevens' law</span
                        > is why bubble sizes get underestimated — scale by area,
                        never radius, and expect readers to undershoot anyway.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >The acuity walk</span
                        > is why labels have a minimum size: design for the back of
                        the room, not the front row.</span
                    >
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                After a morning of measuring these limits on your own visual
                system, the rules stop being someone else's style guide and
                become things you've seen yourself fail at — which is the only
                way design rules ever really stick.
            </p>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                Hope to see you there!
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</Layout>
